A complex $\mathcal K^\bullet$ of sheaves of $\mathcal O$-modules is K-flat when $\mathrm{Tot}(\mathcal A^\bullet\otimes_{\mathcal O}\mathcal K^\bullet)$ is acyclic for every acyclic complex $\mathcal A^\bullet$ (sites-cohomology.tex:2998–3008). A point $p$ of a site gives an exact stalk functor to $\mathcal O_p$-modules. A site has **enough points** when a sheaf map is an isomorphism if its stalks are isomorphisms at all points. Stalkwise K-flatness uses More on Algebra's K-flat definition for complexes of ordinary $\mathcal O_p$-modules.

Let $(\mathcal{C}, \mathcal{O})$ be a ringed site.
Let $\mathcal{K}^\bullet$ be a complex of $\mathcal{O}$-modules.
\begin{enumerate}
\item If $\mathcal{K}^\bullet$ is K-flat, then for every point $p$
of the site $\mathcal{C}$ the complex of $\mathcal{O}_p$-modules
$\mathcal{K}_p^\bullet$ is K-flat in the sense of
More on Algebra, Definition \ref{more-algebra-definition-K-flat}
\item If $\mathcal{C}$ has enough points, then the converse is true.
\end{enumerate}

